title: cwpdoor Installer Fetching Trojanized cwpsrv Binary from 136.0.141.71
id: 6f2c1a94-3d47-4c8e-9b1a-2d5e8f0c7a31
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects the delivery half of the cwpdoor passive backdoor: the installer
    cwpsrv_update.sh (captured by QiAnXin XLab on 2026-06-29) downloads the
    trojanized cwpsrv web server from 136.0.141.71 and overwrites
    /usr/local/cwpsrv/bin/cwpsrv. The backdoor triggers only on requests
    carrying X-Ngx-Integrity-Time/Data/Sig headers validated with an embedded
    RSA key, so the replacement binary is invisible to version checks; this
    rule catches the swap while it happens. No CVE or fixed version exists.
references:
    - https://blog.xlab.qianxin.com/cwpdoor-the-invisible-backdoor-living-inside-control-web-panel/
    - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/hackers-backdoor-linux-servers-using-trojanized-cwp-web-server/
    - https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/hackers-hide-linux-backdoor-in-control.html
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/11/07
tags:
    - attack.t1105
    - attack.t1554
    - attack.t1505
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: linux
detection:
    selection_installer:
        CommandLine|contains:
            - 'cwpsrv_update.sh'
    selection_source_ip:
        CommandLine|contains:
            - '136.0.141.71'
    selection_target_binary:
        CommandLine|contains:
            - '/usr/local/cwpsrv/bin/cwpsrv'
    condition: selection_source_ip or (selection_installer and selection_target_binary)
falsepositives:
    - Legitimate Control Web Panel upgrades that fetch cwpsrv from the vendor's own hosts and run a similarly named updater
    - Administrative scripts that deliberately write the absolute path /usr/local/cwpsrv/bin/cwpsrv
level: high
